Monitoring cotton maturation after harvest-aid application in Xinjiang relies mainly on manual surveys, which cannot readily cover large fields or capture variation within them. This dissertation investigates rapid recognition of defoliation rate and boll opening rate from unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) RGB imagery and deployment on edge devices. To address complex field backgrounds, variable illumination, and limited computing resources, the study develops a dual-label dataset, compares handcrafted features, designs a lightweight recognition network, and constructs low-light synthesis and enhancement methods. Multi-temporal experiments in production fields examine the resulting predictions.

A dual-label dataset links MRK positioning records, RGB images, and manual survey labels from 26 UAV campaigns conducted between September 24 and October 4, 2024, using a custom waypoint-image management tool. Center cropping, rotation and flipping, and normalization produce 8737 image patches of 224×224 pixels. Defoliation rate and boll opening rate have 8 and 6 classes, respectively, with a 5:1:4 training, validation, and test split. In the preliminary pixel-vector experiments, PCA + logistic regression achieves test Macro-F1 scores of 0.30 and 0.40 for the two tasks, exceeding the random forest variants. A further comparison uses a shared split to evaluate pixel inputs against 21-dimensional color and spectral-index statistics. With LR, RF, and SVM classifiers, the structured features achieve best test Macro-F1 scores of 0.909 for defoliation rate and 0.851 for boll opening rate; grade MAE is below 0.3 levels for both tasks.

The proposed Real-Time Cotton Monitoring Network (RTCMNet) combines a shared backbone, multi-scale convolutional attention (MSCA), and two classification heads. It uses 0.37 M parameters and 0.19 GMACs, attaining mean dual-task Accuracy and Macro-F1 of $0.93 plus.minus 0.02$ in five-fold cross-validation. These results are close to DenseNet-121's $0.94 plus.minus 0.01$, with approximately 5.6% of its parameter count. Per-image inference on the DJI remote controller takes 32 ms, compared with 1084 ms for DenseNet-121. Ablation results support the 1,2,2 layer configuration with 8-head MSCA. These scores describe patch-level performance within the target operating domain; generalization across regions, years, and cultivars requires independent evaluation.

For low-light imagery, paired training data are generated by simulating exposure attenuation, white-balance drift, Poisson-Gaussian noise, mild blur, and vignetting in the RGB domain, with parameters calibrated using real nighttime UAV images. The resulting enhancement network, $L^3$-AgriUAVNet, combines HVI representation with an RFDN-S backbone and uses 0.139 M parameters and 6.252 GMACs. It achieves the highest SSIM (0.893) among the compared methods on the paired test set, with a PSNR of 26.24 dB. Tablet inference takes 202 ms per image (4.95 FPS). On a low-light maize-seedling task with detection annotations, enhanced inputs increase YOLOv8n mAP\@0.5 by 14.6% relative to Raw inputs, from 0.1393 to 0.1596. YOLOv8s, however, performs better with Raw inputs, indicating that enhancement should be selected together with the downstream model.

Multi-temporal predictions in production fields L1 and L2, spanning days 0--21 after harvest-aid application, show an overall increase in both indicator grades and spatially continuous variation within the fields. Local predictions in boundary strips with insufficient spray coverage and areas associated with low drip-irrigation pressure correspond to field observations and can guide follow-up surveys. In real sunset cotton imagery, cascading $L^3$-AgriUAVNet with RTCMNet changes the prediction distributions differently across tasks and maturation stages. Without concurrent manual grade labels, these experiments cannot quantify an accuracy gain for the two cotton indicators. The dataset and methods provide a basis for continuous cotton-maturation monitoring and field verification of harvest-aid effects.
